      "Bekaa Kafra, his birthplace": 1,
      "A Lebanese Maronite monk and priest (1828-1898) who lived 23 years as a hermit at Annaya. He was canonized by Paul VI in 1977.": 1,
      "When was Saint Charbel born?": 1,
      "Traditionally on May 8, 1828, in Bekaa Kafra. No authenticated birth record gives the exact day.": 1,
      "Where did Saint Charbel live?": 1,
      "He lived at the Monastery of Our Lady of Mayfouq (novitiate), the Monastery of Saint Maron at Annaya (vows, then community life), the Monastery of Saints Cyprian and Justina at Kfifan (studies), and from 1875 to 1898 at the Hermitage of Saints Peter and Paul above Annaya.": 1,
      "How did Saint Charbel die?": 1,
      "He suffered a stroke on December 16, 1898, while celebrating the Divine Liturgy, and died eight days later, on December 24.": 1,
      "October 9, 1977, by Paul VI, after his beatification on December 5, 1965.": 1,
      "Did Saint Charbel write anything?": 1,
      "Pope Leo XIV called him a man who wrote nothing, in his prayer at the tomb on December 1, 2025.": 1,
      "Is the body of Saint Charbel really incorrupt?": 1,
      "Accounts from the Order and from witnesses say the body was found intact for decades and that a blood-like moisture flowed from it. Reports differ on what later examinations found. The Church canonized him for his life and for healings it approved, not for the condition of the tomb.": 1,
      "Why did Saint Charbel become a hermit?": 1,
      "After ordination he lived 16 years in community at Annaya. In 1875 his superiors permitted him to live in the hermitage of Saints Peter and Paul, where he lived under written rules until his death.": 1,
      "Why are there two feast days?": 1,
      "July 24 is the date in the Roman calendar, an optional memorial. The Maronite calendar keeps the third Sunday of July. December 24 is the anniversary of his death.": 1,
      "Paul VI, homily for the canonization of Charbel Makhlouf, October 9, 1977 (French original)": 1,
      "Paul VI, address for the beatification of Charbel Makhlouf, December 5, 1965": 1,
      "Pope Leo XIV, visit and prayer at the tomb of Saint Charbel, Annaya, December 1, 2025": 1,
      "Guita G. Hourani, \"Saint Sharbel: The Passage to Sainthood,\" Maronite Research Institute (MARI), 1998 - cites the monastery's death record, the hermit rules, the letters received and the tomb openings": 1,
      "Santi e Beati, \"San Charbel (Giuseppe) Makhluf\"": 1,
      "Vatican State, Saint of the Day, July 24: Saint Charbel Makhlouf": 1,
      "Fr. Mansour Awad, \"Three Lights from the East\" (1952), excerpts on charbel.org": 1,
