      "The full story of Saint Charbel: Bekaa Kafra, 16 years in community, 23 as a hermit at Annaya, the tomb, and sainthood. Dated and sourced.": 2,
      "The tomb was opened and the body examined several times in the twentieth century. Santi e Beati (secondary) says that in 1950, after a damp liquid was seen on the tomb wall, the tomb was reopened on February 25 in front of the whole monastic community, and again on April 22 by the religious authorities with a commission of three doctors. MARI reports eight openings between 1950 and 1975 in the presence of doctors and Church representatives, and says the moisture flowed for 67 years from 1899. Other sources, citing the postulator, report that the body was found fully decomposed in 1976. Reports differ, and we say so. We do not make claims about \"no medical explanation\" in our own voice. Those are the statements of the Order and of witnesses.": 1,
      "On December 16, 1898, while saying the Divine Liturgy in the hermitage chapel, Charbel was struck down. The Order's accounts say it happened while reciting a liturgical prayer (\"Father of truth, behold your Son, a sacrifice pleasing to you...\") and that he kept his hands clasped around the Eucharist as he fell. His companion, Father Makarios Al-Mishmeshani, and other monks helped him to his cell. The prayer fragment is an English rendering of the quoted historical account, not an authorized liturgical translation.": 1,
      "The Dicastery for the Causes of Saints describes Charbel Makhlouf as a priest of the Lebanese Maronite Order who withdrew from the monastery to a hermitage in search of austere solitude and came to rest in the Lord on December 24.": 1,
      "Documented in Church sources (Paul VI's 1977 homily and the Dicastery for the Causes of Saints)": 1,
